        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "2577d83f-5dbf-5892-909d-d7d346ac4218",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Organizations: Kazan School, Prague School of structural linguistics, Michigan Slavic Publications\n- People: Jan Baudouin de Courtenay, Nikolai Trubetzkoy, Ladislav Matejka, Roman Jakobson\n- Places: Kazan, Moscow, Vienna\n- Topics: linguistics, structural linguistics, Slavic publications\n\nFacts:\n- Kazan School was an influential group of linguists in Kazan.\n- The linguistic circle of Kazan School included the Polish linguist Jan Baudouin de Courtenay and his student Nikolai Trubetzkoy.\n- Prince Nikolai Sergeyevich Trubetzkoy was born in Moscow on April 16, 1890, and he died in Vienna on June 25, 1938.\n- Nikolai Trubetzkoy was a Russian linguist and historian whose teachings formed a nucleus of the Prague School of structural linguistics.\n- In 1962, Ladislav Matejka founded Michigan Slavic Publications.\n- Michigan Slavic Publications has published more than 100 volumes by authors such as Roman Jakobson and Nikolai Trubetzkoy.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
